      "title": "Alphabet slides 8% on slower Search growth while Microsoft's cloud accelerates",
      "topics": [
        "Tech",
        "Earnings",
        "AI"
      ],
      "facts": [
        "Alphabet's Q3 revenue rose 25% to $127.6bn, slightly below analyst expectations. Net income fell to $26.17bn from $34.9bn a year ago.",
        "Search ad revenue grew about 12% to $63.35bn, down from 17% growth in Q2 and 19% in Q1. Analysts expected about $65bn, and the shares fell around 8–9% after hours.",
        "Google Cloud more than doubled, up 103% to $30.7bn, and Pichai says Alphabet is now on a half-trillion-dollar annual revenue run rate. Capex rose to about $51bn in the quarter.",
        "Google is taking ads in AI Mode to international markets for the first time, though its business chief admits they still monetize worse than classic search.",
        "Microsoft's fiscal Q1 brought $92.3bn in revenue, $63.9bn in Microsoft Cloud revenue (growth accelerating to 30%, per Nadella) and 48% Azure growth.",
        "A filing shows $10bn of that quarter came from commercial arrangements with OpenAI, nearly 11% of the total, and Microsoft's OpenAI stake slipped to about 24%."
      ],
      "whyItMatters": "Investors are rewarding AI cloud demand but punishing any sign that AI answers are eroding Google's core search ad machine.",
